 ## Background
-The concept of line illustration in volume visualization has roots in traditional printmaking, specifically copper plate etching, which was used for centuries in scientific publications before modern photographic reproduction [S1, unofficial]. Leister simulated this process by intersecting scenes with parallel planes and using a modified ray tracer [S1, unofficial].
-
-In the context of digital volume visualization, Interrante introduced a novel line illustration technique for visualizing volume isosurfaces in 1997 [S1, unofficial]. This work focused on using principal directions and curvatures to advect particles, creating viewpoint-independent line textures that could visualize multiple isosurfaces simultaneously [S1, unofficial].
-
-The distinction between analytical and pixel-based methods is well-documented in surveys of NPR techniques. Analytical methods are noted for their flexibility and lack of resolution dependence, while pixel-based methods (like those by Saito and Takahashi) are favored for their speed and simplicity when analytical models are unavailable [S1, unofficial]. Hertzmann provided a survey of image-space outline generation algorithms, noting that simple outlines can be generated by extracting edges from depth maps [S1, unofficial].
+The concept of line illustration in volume visualization has roots in traditional printmaking, specifically copper plate etching, which was used for centuries in scientific publications before modern photographic reproduction [S1, unofficial]. Leister simulated this process by intersecting scenes with parallel planes and using a modified ray tracer [S1, unofficial]. In the context of digital volume visualization, Interrante introduced a novel line illustration technique for visualizing volume isosurfaces in 1997 [S1, unofficial]. This work focused on using principal directions and curvatures to advect particles, creating viewpoint-independent line textures that could visualize multiple isosurfaces simultaneously [S1, unofficial]. The distinction between analytical and pixel-based methods is well-documented in surveys of NPR techniques. Analytical methods are noted for their flexibility and lack of resolution dependence, while pixel-based methods (like those by Saito and Takahashi) are favored for their speed and simplicity when analytical models are unavailable [S1, unofficial]. Hertzmann provided a survey of image-space outline generation algorithms, noting that simple outlines can be generated by extracting edges from depth maps [S1, unofficial].
 
